KLIN SERVER-SENT EVENTS
-----------------------

A page can stream events to the browser (the HTML EventSource API,
"text/event-stream"). Include <klin/sse.h>:

    <%! #include <klin/sse.h> %><%
        if(sse_start(request, response))
            return;

        /* wait for my_fd (a pipe, a socket...) with a 15 s keep-alive */
        for(;;)
        {
            int w = sse_wait(request, my_fd, 15000);

            if(w == SSE_CLOSED)
                break;                      /* the client went away */
            if(w == SSE_TIMEOUT && sse_comment(response, "ping"))
                break;
            if(w == SSE_READY && sse_send(response, "update", id, data))
                break;
        }
    %>

Put nothing before the code block, not even a line break: sse_start()
sends the header, and the page's output goes to the client as written.

- sse_start(rq, rs): Content-Type text/event-stream, Cache-Control
  no-store, X-Accel-Buffering no (proxies that buffer, e.g. nginx, pass
  events on at once); sends the header now.
- sse_send(rs, event, id, data): one event. event (type) and id are one
  line each, or NULL; data may hold line breaks (each line becomes a
  "data:" line, and the browser gets the line breaks back).
- sse_comment(rs, text): a comment line, to keep an idle stream open
  through proxies that close silent connections.
- sse_retry(rs, ms): how long the browser waits before reconnecting.
- sse_last_event_id(rq): the id of the last event the browser got, sent
  when it reconnects (Last-Event-ID), so the page can go on from there.
- sse_wait(rq, fd, ms): wait until fd (-1 for none) is readable
  (SSE_READY), ms milliseconds pass (SSE_TIMEOUT, -1 for no limit) or the
  client closes the connection (SSE_CLOSED). It waits in poll(2) on the
  client socket and fd, and runs the timer's alarms meanwhile.

Every send returns non-zero when the client is gone: stop streaming. A
send the client does not take within 30 s closes the connection.

On HTTP/1.1 the stream is chunked and the connection can be reused when
the page ends; on HTTP/1.0 the end of the stream closes it. TLS works the
same way.

A stream holds its connection, and so a whole worker, for as long as it
lasts. In the prefork model the watchdog asks the parent for another
worker after a second (as for any slow page), and the streaming worker
exits when its stream ends; size prefork.max_child for the number of
streams expected. The single-process (-d) server serves nothing else
while a stream is open.
